# Batch 24 (2026-11-11): ancient Near East & Mediterranean wonders and puzzles; South & East Asian epics, myths and temples;
# Africa (southern Africa), the Maya world, European legends and vampire panics; modern anomalies, poltergeists, ghost ships and dating science
H,M='history','myth'
def X(id,title,lane,status,pr,start,ev,main,alts,src,img=None,end=None,changed=None,dateNote=None):
  d=dict(id=id,title=title,lane=lane,status=status,priority=pr,start=start,end=end,ev=ev,main=main,alts=alts,src=src,img=img)
  if changed:d['changed']=changed
  if dateNote:d['dateNote']=dateNote
  return d
E=[]
# --- part 1: ancient Near East & Mediterranean
E+=[
X('atrahasis','Atrahasis: the Babylonian flood story where noisy humans anger the gods',M,'myth-legend',2,-1700,['textual'],
 "The Atrahasis epic is an Akkadian poem known from Old Babylonian tablets of roughly the 17th century BCE, with later Assyrian and Babylonian copies. In it the lesser gods rebel against digging canals, so the gods create humans from clay mixed with the flesh and blood of a slain god to do the work. Humanity multiplies and its noise keeps the god Enlil awake; he sends plague, drought and famine, and finally a great flood. The wise god Enki warns the man Atrahasis to build a boat, and he survives with his family and animals. Afterwards the gods set limits on human numbers, such as infertility and infant death. The flood section closely parallels Tablet XI of the Epic of Gilgamesh and has many similarities with the Genesis flood narrative.",
 [('Which story came first?','Most scholars see Atrahasis (and the Sumerian Ziusudra story) as older than the written Genesis account and as one source of a shared Mesopotamian flood tradition. Some religious commentators instead see the stories as independent memories of a real worldwide event.'),
  ('A real flood behind it?','Researchers have linked Mesopotamian flood stories to destructive local river floods, such as flood layers excavated at Ur, Shuruppak and Kish, or to wider events such as post-glacial sea-level rise. None of these is proven to be the specific source.'),
  ('Ancient astronaut reading','Ancient-astronaut writers such as Zecharia Sitchin read the story of gods creating workers as genetic engineering by the Anunnaki; Assyriologists reject these translations. This view is speculative.')],
 ['wiki:Atra-Hasis','wiki:Flood_myth'],img='Atra-Hasis',dateNote='oldest copies c. 17th century BCE'),
X('ishtar-gate','The Ishtar Gate of Babylon and its dragons (c. 575 BCE)',H,'confirmed',2,-575,['archaeological','textual'],
 "The Ishtar Gate was the eighth gate of the inner city of Babylon, built around 575 BCE on the orders of King Nebuchadnezzar II. Its walls were faced with glazed blue bricks showing rows of aurochs and mušḫuššu (a scaly dragon with a snake's head, lion forelegs and bird hind legs, sacred to the god Marduk), while lions lined the Processional Way leading to it. German archaeologist Robert Koldewey excavated it between 1902 and 1914, and a reconstruction using the original bricks was opened in the Pergamon Museum in Berlin in the 1930s. Other panels are in museums around the world, and a smaller replica stands at the site in Iraq.",
 [('Mainstream: a composite beast','Historians of Mesopotamian art see the mušḫuššu as a mythical composite creature, a symbol of Marduk, comparable to griffins and sphinxes.'),
  ('A dinosaur memory?','Some cryptozoologists and young-Earth creationists have suggested the mušḫuššu depicts a living animal such as a sauropod, sometimes linking it to African reports of Mokele-mbembe. No zoological or palaeontological evidence supports this; it is speculative.'),
  ('Who owns the gate?','Iraq has called for the return of the gate material held in Berlin, while the museum states it was legally divided and exported under the agreements of the time.')],
 ['wiki:Ishtar_Gate','wiki:Mušḫuššu'],img='Ishtar_Gate'),
X('rosetta-stone','The Rosetta Stone: the key that unlocked hieroglyphs (196 BCE / 1822)',H,'confirmed',1,-196,['archaeological','textual'],
 "The Rosetta Stone is a granodiorite slab carrying a decree issued at Memphis in 196 BCE on behalf of King Ptolemy V, written three times: in Egyptian hieroglyphs, in Demotic script and in ancient Greek. French soldiers found it in July 1799 near the town of Rashid (Rosetta) during Napoleon's campaign in Egypt. After the French defeat it passed to Britain under the Capitulation of Alexandria in 1801 and has been in the British Museum since 1802. Because the Greek text could be read, scholars could compare the scripts. Thomas Young made important early progress with the Demotic and the royal names in cartouches, and Jean-François Champollion announced in 1822 that hieroglyphs combined sound signs with idea signs, which opened up Egyptian texts.",
 [('Who deserves the credit?','Champollion is usually credited with the decipherment, but Young\'s supporters argued he identified key sound values first. Most historians now see Young\'s work as a real but partial contribution and Champollion\'s system as the breakthrough.'),
  ('Should it go back to Egypt?','Egyptian archaeologists, including former antiquities minister Zahi Hawass, and petitions have called for its return; the British Museum maintains it was legally acquired under the 1801 treaty.'),
  ('Older ideas about hieroglyphs','Before decipherment, Renaissance and early modern scholars such as Athanasius Kircher believed hieroglyphs were purely symbolic, mystical writing hiding secret wisdom. The stone showed they also wrote ordinary language, including tax and priestly matters.')],
 ['wiki:Rosetta_Stone','wiki:Decipherment_of_ancient_Egyptian_scripts'],img='Rosetta_Stone',
 changed="Before 1822 hieroglyphs were widely seen as mystical symbols; after Champollion they could be read as a language, transforming knowledge of ancient Egypt."),
X('galilee-boat','The Galilee Boat ("Jesus boat"), found in a drought (1986)',H,'confirmed',3,-40,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "In January 1986, during a severe drought that lowered the Sea of Galilee, brothers Moshe and Yuval Lufan noticed the outline of an ancient boat in the mud near Kibbutz Ginosar in Israel. Archaeologists excavated it in a rapid 12-day operation and preserved it in polyethylene glycol. The 8.2 m wooden fishing boat was built from several kinds of timber and repaired many times. Radiocarbon dating and the pottery and nails found with it place it between about the 1st century BCE and the 1st century CE. It is displayed at the Yigal Allon Centre at Ginosar.",
 [('Connection to Jesus?','Because it dates to roughly the time of Jesus and comes from the lake where the Gospels set many events, it was nicknamed the Jesus boat. Archaeologists stress there is no evidence linking it to Jesus or his disciples; it shows the kind of boat used on the lake then.'),
  ('A window on everyday life','For historians its value is in what it shows about ordinary fishing boats and shortages of good timber, as it reused wood from older vessels.')],
 ['wiki:Sea_of_Galilee_Boat'],img='Sea_of_Galilee_Boat',dateNote='built c. 1st century BCE–1st century CE; found 1986'),
X('colossus-of-rhodes','The Colossus of Rhodes: a wonder that stood for only 54 years',H,'confirmed',2,-280,['textual'],
 "The Colossus of Rhodes was a bronze statue of the sun god Helios, about 33 m tall, built by the sculptor Chares of Lindos to celebrate the city's survival of a siege by Demetrius Poliorcetes in 305–304 BCE. It was completed around 280 BCE and stood near the harbour of Rhodes until an earthquake toppled it around 226 BCE. Pliny the Elder wrote that even lying on the ground it was a marvel, as few people could wrap their arms around its thumb. The ruins reportedly lay for about 800 years until Arab forces sold the bronze in the 7th century CE. It is listed among the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World.",
 [('Did it straddle the harbour?','The famous image of the statue standing with legs astride the harbour entrance comes from medieval and later artists. Modern engineers and historians consider this impossible for a bronze statue of that era, and the harbour would have had to be closed for years during construction; most think it stood on a pedestal on land.'),
  ('The 900 camels','The story that a merchant needed 900 camels to carry away the scrap comes from the Byzantine chronicler Theophanes, writing long after the event; some historians doubt the details.'),
  ('Where exactly?','Its exact site is unknown. Proposals include the harbour mole and the acropolis of Rhodes near the temple of Helios; no foundation has been firmly identified.')],
 ['wiki:Colossus_of_Rhodes','wiki:Seven_Wonders_of_the_Ancient_World'],img='Colossus_of_Rhodes',end=-226),
X('lighthouse-of-alexandria','The Pharos of Alexandria: the lighthouse of the ancient world',H,'confirmed',2,-280,['textual','archaeological','numismatic'],
 "The Lighthouse of Alexandria stood on the island of Pharos at the entrance to Alexandria's harbour. It was begun under Ptolemy I and completed around 280 BCE under Ptolemy II, with its dedication attributed to Sostratus of Cnidus. Estimates of its height range around 100 m or more, making it one of the tallest human-made structures of its time; coins and descriptions show a three-tiered tower with a statue on top. It was damaged by a series of earthquakes between the 10th and 14th centuries and was ruined by about 1323. In 1477 the Mamluk sultan Qaitbay built a fort on its site, partly from its stones. Divers led by Jean-Yves Empereur documented large blocks and statues in the harbour from 1994.",
 [('The burning mirror','Medieval Arab writers told of a huge mirror at the top that could spot ships far out at sea or even set them on fire. Historians treat the burning mirror as legend, though a polished reflector to project the fire\'s light is plausible.'),
  ('Rebuilding plans','Egyptian authorities and others have periodically proposed rebuilding the lighthouse or creating an underwater museum at the site; these remain proposals.'),
  ('Word legacy','The name Pharos became the word for lighthouse in several languages, such as French phare, Italian and Spanish faro.')],
 ['wiki:Lighthouse_of_Alexandria'],img='Lighthouse_of_Alexandria',end=1323),
X('minoan-snake-goddess','The Minoan snake goddesses: ancient figurines and modern forgeries',H,'disputed',3,-1600,['archaeological'],
 "In 1903 Arthur Evans found faience figurines of women holding snakes in the Temple Repositories at the palace of Knossos on Crete, dated to about 1600 BCE. They became famous as the Minoan snake goddess, though one was found headless and was restored with a head and a cat-like animal on top, and the other is missing parts. Evans saw them as evidence of a Minoan mother goddess religion. In the following decades, several ivory and gold snake goddess figurines without secure find spots appeared on the antiquities market, including one bought by the Museum of Fine Arts in Boston in 1914.",
 [('Goddess, priestess or votary?','Scholars debate whether the Knossos figures show a goddess, priestesses or worshippers; the label goddess comes from Evans and his interest in a supreme female deity.'),
  ('Forgeries','Archaeologist Kenneth Lapatin\'s 2002 study argued that the Boston ivory figurine and several similar pieces are modern forgeries, possibly made by craftsmen who worked on Evans\'s restorations. The Boston museum itself now presents its figure with doubts about authenticity.'),
  ('How much is restoration?','Critics point out that much of the popular image of Minoan Crete, from frescoes to these figurines, was shaped by Evans\'s reconstructions, which reflected early 20th-century ideas.')],
 ['wiki:Minoan_snake_goddess_figurines','wiki:Arthur_Evans'],img='Minoan_snake_goddess_figurines',dateNote='c. 1600 BCE; found 1903'),
X('nimrud-lens','The Nimrud lens: an Assyrian telescope?',H,'disputed',3,-750,['archaeological'],
 "The Nimrud lens is a piece of polished rock crystal, about 4 cm across, found in 1850 by Austen Henry Layard in the palace of Nimrud in present-day Iraq. It dates from about the 8th or 7th century BCE and is now in the British Museum. It is slightly oval and ground to a convex shape, and it magnifies somewhat, though unevenly. Similar polished crystals have been found at other ancient sites.",
 [('Mainstream: inlay or burning glass','Many archaeologists think it was a decorative inlay, perhaps for furniture, though it could also have served as a magnifying glass or to focus sunlight to start fires.'),
  ('A telescope?','In 1999 Assyriologist Giovanni Pettinato proposed that it was used by Assyrian astronomers, which might explain their knowledge of the heavens. Optical studies found it too irregular to work as a useful telescope lens, and no other part of a telescope has been found; most scholars do not accept this idea.'),
  ('Ancient optics more broadly','The lens is often cited in discussions of ancient technology alongside Roman magnifying glass references (Seneca, Pliny) and Viking-age Visby lenses.')],
 ['wiki:Nimrud_lens'],img='Nimrud_lens',dateNote='c. 750–710 BCE; found 1850'),
X('cyclops-dwarf-elephants','Cyclops and the skulls of dwarf elephants',H,'hypothesis',3,-700,['palaeontological','textual','speculative'],
 "During the Pleistocene, elephants that reached Mediterranean islands such as Sicily, Malta, Crete and Cyprus evolved into dwarf species, some only about 1 m tall. Their skulls have a single large central opening for the trunk, which can look like one huge eye socket to someone unfamiliar with elephants. In 1914 the palaeontologist Othenio Abel suggested that ancient Greeks who found such skulls may have taken them for the remains of one-eyed giants, the Cyclopes known from Homer's Odyssey (around the 8th century BCE), which places the Cyclops Polyphemus on an island often identified with Sicily.",
 [('Fossil folklore','Historian of science Adrienne Mayor\'s The First Fossil Hunters (2000) argues that ancient Greeks and Romans did collect and interpret large fossil bones as remains of giants and heroes, which makes this idea plausible.'),
  ('Unproven link','No ancient text describes a skull of this kind as a Cyclops, so the connection remains a hypothesis. The Cyclops may also come from storytelling motifs found widely around the world.'),
  ('Other sources of giants','Other suggestions link Greek giant legends to mammoth and mastodon bones found on the mainland, and the griffin to Protoceratops fossils in Central Asia, also proposed by Mayor and debated by palaeontologists.')],
 ['wiki:Dwarf_elephant','wiki:Polyphemus','wiki:Cyclopes'],img='Dwarf_elephant',dateNote='Odyssey c. 8th century BCE; hypothesis 1914'),
X('tall-el-hammam','Tall el-Hammam: Sodom and a cosmic airburst?',H,'disputed',2,-1650,['archaeological','geological','textual','speculative'],
 "Tall el-Hammam is a large Bronze Age settlement in the Jordan Valley, north-east of the Dead Sea in Jordan. Excavations led by Steven Collins since 2005 found that it was destroyed around 1650 BCE. In 2021 a team led by Ted Bunch published a paper in Scientific Reports arguing that a cosmic airburst, an exploding meteor like the one at Tunguska, destroyed the city, citing melted pottery, shocked quartz and scattered human bones. The paper was retracted by the journal in April 2025 (see below). The authors had suggested that memories of the event might lie behind the biblical story of Sodom and Gomorrah destroyed by fire from heaven.",
 [('The airburst paper was retracted','Other researchers, including Jaret and Harris (2022) and Boslough and Bruno (2025), reported errors in the mineral and geochemical analysis and argued that the comparison with Tunguska overestimated that blast. After author corrections in 2022 and 2023, the journal\'s editors retracted the paper in April 2025, saying they no longer had confidence in its conclusions. Eleven of the co-authors stated that they disagree with the retraction.'),
  ('Is it Sodom?','Collins identifies Tall el-Hammam with biblical Sodom. Most archaeologists and biblical scholars do not accept this identification, noting differences in dates and geography and the difficulty of matching legends to specific sites.'),
  ('Part of a wider debate','The study comes from researchers associated with the Younger Dryas impact hypothesis, which is also contested; both remain under debate.')],
 ['wiki:Tall_el-Hammam','wiki:Sodom_and_Gomorrah','doi:10.1038/s41598-021-97778-3','doi:10.1038/s41598-025-99265-5','doi:10.1038/s41598-022-08216-x'],img='Tall_el-Hammam',dateNote='destroyed c. 1650 BCE; airburst paper 2021, retracted 2025'),
]
# --- part 2: South & East Asia
E+=[
X('ramayana','The Ramayana: Rama, Sita, Hanuman and a bridge to Lanka',M,'myth-legend',1,-500,['textual','oral tradition'],
 "The Ramayana is a Sanskrit epic traditionally attributed to the sage Valmiki. Scholars think it was composed in stages, with core books perhaps from around the 5th to 4th century BCE and later additions into the early centuries CE. It tells how Prince Rama of Ayodhya is exiled, how his wife Sita is abducted by Ravana, the king of Lanka, and how Rama rescues her with the help of his brother Lakshmana and an army of monkeys and bears led by Hanuman, after building a bridge across the sea. It became one of the most influential stories in Asia, retold in hundreds of versions such as Tulsidas's Ramcharitmanas, the Thai Ramakien, the Khmer Reamker and the Javanese Kakawin Ramayana, and in dance, puppetry and temple carvings from India to Indonesia.",
 [('Myth or history?','Many Hindus regard Rama as a historical figure and an avatar of Vishnu who lived in the Treta Yuga, an earlier world age. Historians treat the epic as literature built over centuries, though it may preserve memories of real places and kingdoms in ancient India.'),
  ('Rama\'s Bridge','The chain of shoals between India and Sri Lanka, called Ram Setu or Adam\'s Bridge, is identified by many believers with the bridge in the epic. Geologists describe it as a natural formation of sand and coral shoals.'),
  ('Flying chariots','Ravana\'s flying chariot, the Pushpaka vimana, is cited by some modern writers as evidence of ancient aircraft. Scholars read it as a mythological vehicle of the gods; the aircraft claim is speculative.')],
 ['wiki:Ramayana','wiki:Versions_of_the_Ramayana'],img='Ramayana',dateNote='composed c. 5th century BCE–early centuries CE'),
X('change-moon-goddess',"Chang'e: the woman who flew to the Moon",M,'myth-legend',2,-200,['textual','oral tradition'],
 "In Chinese mythology Chang'e is the wife of the archer Hou Yi, who shot down nine of the ten suns. In the best-known versions, Hou Yi receives an elixir of immortality from the Queen Mother of the West; Chang'e drinks it, alone or to keep it from a thief, and floats up to the Moon, where she lives in the Moon Palace with a jade rabbit pounding medicine. Early references appear in the Huainanzi (2nd century BCE) and in the Guizang divination text known from bamboo slips excavated at Wangjiatai. The story is closely tied to the Mid-Autumn Festival. China's lunar exploration programme is named after her: Chang'e 4 made the first soft landing on the Moon's far side in January 2019, carrying the Yutu-2 (Jade Rabbit 2) rover, and Chang'e 6 returned the first samples from the far side in 2024.",
 [('Different versions','In some early versions Chang\'e is punished by being turned into a toad on the Moon, reflecting the old Chinese image of a toad in the Moon; later versions make her a graceful, lonely immortal.'),
  ('Moon patterns','The rabbit and toad in the Moon are interpretations of the dark lunar maria. A rabbit in the Moon also appears in Indian, Japanese, Korean and Mesoamerican traditions, a case of similar pareidolia in different cultures.')],
 ["wiki:Chang'e",'wiki:Chinese_Lunar_Exploration_Program','wiki:Moon_rabbit'],img="Chang'e",dateNote='recorded from c. 2nd century BCE'),
X('penglai','Penglai: the island of the immortals that emperors tried to reach',M,'myth-legend',3,-219,['textual','speculative'],
 "Penglai is a mythical mountain-island in the eastern sea where, in Chinese legend, immortals live in palaces of gold and jade and the herb of immortality grows. It is one of the three holy islands, with Fangzhang and Yingzhou, described in texts such as the Shiji of Sima Qian. According to the Shiji, the First Emperor of Qin sent the alchemist Xu Fu with ships and young men and women to find it in 219 BCE, and Emperor Wu of Han later sponsored more searches. The coastal city of Penglai in Shandong province takes its name from the legend.",
 [('Mirages as inspiration?','The coast near Penglai is known for sea mirages (superior mirages of distant islands and coasts). Several writers have suggested such mirages helped inspire the idea of floating islands of immortals; this is plausible but not provable.'),
  ('Did Xu Fu reach Japan?','A later tradition, strong in Japan, holds that Xu Fu reached Japan and stayed; historians consider this legend rather than history.'),
  ('Paradise patterns','Penglai fits a worldwide pattern of distant paradise islands, like the Celtic Isles of the Blessed, Avalon and Hy-Brasil.')],
 ['wiki:Penglai','wiki:Xu_Fu'],img='Penglai_Pavilion',dateNote='Xu Fu expedition 219 BCE'),
X('shennong','Shennong, the Divine Farmer who tasted every herb',M,'myth-legend',3,-2737,['textual','oral tradition'],
 "Shennong (the Divine Farmer) is a legendary ruler of ancient China, counted among the Three Sovereigns, who is said to have taught people to farm with the plough and to have tested hundreds of plants on himself to find which were medicines and which were poisons, sometimes being poisoned dozens of times in a day. The classic herbal Shennong Bencao Jing, which lists 365 medicinal substances, bears his name but was compiled around the Han dynasty (roughly the 1st–2nd century CE). A popular later legend says he discovered tea in 2737 BCE when leaves blew into his boiling water; this date is traditional, not historical.",
 [('Historical person?','Historians see Shennong as a mythological culture hero, possibly a personification of the beginnings of agriculture in China, rather than a real king.'),
  ('Real early farming','Archaeology shows millet farming in northern China and rice farming in the Yangtze region from roughly 9,000–7,000 years ago, much earlier than the traditional date for Shennong.'),
  ('Civilising teachers','Shennong belongs to a worldwide group of figures who teach farming or crafts, such as Osiris, Quetzalcoatl and Oannes; some alternative writers read this pattern as memory of real visiting teachers, which is speculative.')],
 ['wiki:Shennong','wiki:Shennong_Ben_Cao_Jing'],img='Shennong',dateNote='traditional date 2737 BCE (legendary)'),
X('yamato-takeru','Yamato Takeru: the prince who became a white bird',M,'myth-legend',3,712,['textual'],
 "Yamato Takeru is a legendary prince of Japan, son of Emperor Keikō, whose adventures are told in the Kojiki (712) and the Nihon Shoki (720). He defeats the Kumaso chiefs in the west disguised as a woman and campaigns against the Emishi in the east. His aunt, the priestess of Ise, gives him the sword Kusanagi, with which he cuts the grass to escape a fire set by enemies. After offending the god of Mount Ibuki he falls ill and dies, and his soul flies away as a great white bird. The sword Kusanagi became one of the Three Sacred Treasures of the Japanese imperial regalia.",
 [('Real person or composite?','Historians generally see him as a legendary figure who may combine the deeds of several generals of the early Yamato state, used to explain its expansion; his historicity cannot be confirmed.'),
  ('The sacred sword today','Kusanagi is said to be kept at Atsuta Shrine in Nagoya, but it is never shown, so whether an ancient sword exists there is unknown.')],
 ['wiki:Yamato_Takeru','wiki:Kusanagi_no_Tsurugi'],img='Yamato_Takeru',dateNote='recorded 712 and 720; set in the legendary early Yamato period'),
X('ajanta-caves','The Ajanta Caves, rediscovered by a tiger hunter (1819)',H,'confirmed',2,-200,['archaeological','art historical'],
 "The Ajanta Caves are about 30 Buddhist monuments cut into a horseshoe-shaped cliff above the Waghur river in Maharashtra, India. The earliest were made around the 2nd century BCE; most of the rest were carved and painted in a burst of activity in the 5th century CE under the Vakataka dynasty. Their wall paintings of the Buddha's lives and Jataka tales are among the finest surviving examples of ancient Indian painting. The caves were abandoned and overgrown until April 1819, when a British cavalry officer, John Smith, came upon them while hunting a tiger and scratched his name on a painting in Cave 10. They became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1983.",
 [('How long did the second phase take?','Art historian Walter Spink argued that most of the later caves were made in an intense period of under 20 years, roughly 460–480 CE; other scholars favour a longer timescale.'),
  ('Lost to whom?','The caves were lost to outsiders, but local people likely knew of them; the word rediscovery reflects the colonial record.')],
 ['wiki:Ajanta_Caves'],img='Ajanta_Caves',end=480,dateNote='c. 2nd century BCE and c. 460–480 CE; rediscovered 1819'),
X('todaiji-great-buddha','The Great Buddha of Tōdai-ji, Nara (752)',H,'confirmed',2,752,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Tōdai-ji in Nara was founded by Emperor Shōmu, who in 743 ordered a giant bronze statue of the Vairocana Buddha after years of epidemic, famine and rebellion. Casting took place from about 745 to 749 and consumed a large share of Japan's copper, tin and gold. The eye-opening ceremony in 752 was performed by the Indian monk Bodhisena, with guests from across Asia. The statue, about 15 m tall, has been damaged by earthquakes and fires and repaired many times; its present head dates from the 17th century, and the current hall (Daibutsuden) was rebuilt in 1709. The temple's treasure house, the Shōsōin, preserves thousands of 8th-century objects, some brought along the Silk Road.",
 [('A building with a toxic cost?','Gilding the statue used gold dissolved in mercury, which was then evaporated by heating. Some researchers have suggested that mercury vapour may have caused illness in Nara; this remains a hypothesis.'),
  ('State and religion','Historians see the project as both an act of devotion and a statement of imperial power, linking a network of provincial temples to Nara.')],
 ['wiki:Tōdai-ji','wiki:Shōsōin'],img='Tōdai-ji',dateNote='cast 745–749, consecrated 752'),
X('priest-king-mohenjo-daro','The Priest-King of Mohenjo-daro: a famous label for a mystery',H,'confirmed',3,-2200,['archaeological'],
 "The so-called Priest-King is a small steatite sculpture of a bearded man, about 17.5 cm high, found in 1927 at Mohenjo-daro, one of the largest cities of the Indus (Harappan) civilisation, now in Pakistan. It dates from roughly 2400–1900 BCE. The man wears a headband and an armband and a robe decorated with trefoil (three-lobed) patterns that were once filled with red pigment. It is in the National Museum of Pakistan in Karachi and is one of the best-known images of the Indus civilisation.",
 [('Priest, king or neither?','The name was given by early excavators by analogy with Mesopotamia and Egypt. Archaeologists note that the Indus cities show no obvious palaces, royal tombs or large temples, so it is unknown whether there were priest-kings at all; the figure might be an ancestor, an elite person or a deity.'),
  ('Undeciphered script','Because the Indus script remains undeciphered, the identities and titles of people like this remain uncertain.')],
 ['wiki:Priest-King_(sculpture)','wiki:Mohenjo-daro'],img='Priest-King_(sculpture)',dateNote='c. 2400–1900 BCE; found 1927'),
X('ta-prohm-stegosaurus','The "stegosaurus" carving of Ta Prohm, Cambodia',H,'disputed',3,1186,['archaeological','speculative'],
 "Ta Prohm is a Khmer temple near Angkor, built from 1186 by King Jayavarman VII as a Buddhist monastery and famous for the trees growing over its ruins. Among the many small carved roundels on its doorframes is one showing a four-legged animal with a row of rounded shapes along its back. Since the late 1990s some writers, especially young-Earth creationists, have claimed it depicts a stegosaurus and shows that people saw living dinosaurs about 800 years ago.",
 [('Mainstream view','Archaeologists and scholars of Khmer art note that the shapes along the back match the leafy background decoration found around other roundels on the temple, and that the animal could be a rhinoceros, boar, chameleon or a mythological creature, since the temple is full of religious and imaginary beings. They see no evidence of a living dinosaur.'),
  ('Is it modern?','Some sceptics have suggested the carving might be a modern addition; others reply that there is no evidence of this. Most commentators accept it as original but read it as an ordinary animal.'),
  ('Dinosaur claims elsewhere','Similar claims of dinosaurs in ancient art include the Ica stones and Acámbaro figures; these are generally considered hoaxes or misinterpretations.')],
 ['wiki:Dinosaur_of_Ta_Prohm','wiki:Ta_Prohm','url:https://skepticalinquirer.org/2019/07/a-stegosaur-carving-on-the-ruins-of-ta-prohm-think-again/|Skeptical Inquirer: A Stegosaur Carving on the Ruins of Ta Prohm? Think Again (2019)|article'],img='Ta_Prohm',dateNote='temple from 1186; claim since 1990s'),
X('chola-srivijaya-1025','The Chola navy raids Srivijaya (1025)',H,'confirmed',3,1025,['textual','epigraphic'],
 "In 1025 the south Indian Chola emperor Rajendra I sent a naval expedition across the Bay of Bengal against the maritime empire of Srivijaya, which controlled the Strait of Malacca. Chola inscriptions, including the Tanjore (Thanjavur) inscription, list ports raided in Sumatra and the Malay Peninsula, such as Kadaram (Kedah), and record the capture of the Srivijayan king Sangrama Vijayatunggavarman. The raid weakened Srivijaya and is one of the few overseas military expeditions by an Indian state before modern times.",
 [('Why attack?','Historians suggest the aim was to protect Chola and Tamil merchants and open the sea routes to China, rather than to conquer land; the Cholas did not keep lasting control.'),
  ('How big?','The inscriptions list places but give no numbers; the scale of the fleet is unknown.')],
 ['wiki:Chola_invasion_of_Srivijaya','wiki:Rajendra_I'],img='Rajendra_I'),
]
# --- part 3: Africa, the Maya world, the American West and European legends
E+=[
X('hannibal-alps','Hannibal crosses the Alps with elephants (218 BCE)',H,'confirmed',1,-218,['textual','archaeological','environmental'],
 "In 218 BCE, at the start of the Second Punic War, the Carthaginian general Hannibal led an army of tens of thousands of soldiers, cavalry and about 37 war elephants from Spain over the Pyrenees and the Alps into Italy, to attack Rome from the north. The crossing in late autumn cost him many men and animals to cold, landslides and attacks by mountain tribes, but he went on to win great victories at the Trebia, Lake Trasimene and Cannae (216 BCE). He stayed in Italy for about 15 years but never took Rome, and Carthage was defeated at Zama in 202 BCE. The main ancient accounts are by Polybius and Livy.",
 [('Which pass?','The route has been argued over since antiquity, with candidates including the Col du Mont Cenis, Col du Clapier, Col du Montgenèvre, Little St Bernard and Col de la Traversette.'),
  ('Evidence in the mud','In 2016–2017 a team led by William Mahaney reported a disturbed layer of soil near the Col de la Traversette containing large amounts of microbes typical of horse dung, radiocarbon dated to around 200 BCE, and proposed it marks Hannibal\'s army. Other researchers consider this suggestive but not conclusive.'),
  ('How many elephants survived?','Polybius says nearly all the elephants died after the battle of the Trebia that winter, leaving only one. A Roman tradition preserved by Pliny names a brave Carthaginian elephant Surus, and some writers identify it with that survivor; this is uncertain.')],
 ["wiki:Hannibal's_crossing_of_the_Alps",'doi:10.1111/arcm.12231'],img='Hannibal',dateNote='autumn 218 BCE'),
X('impundulu','The impundulu: the lightning bird of southern Africa',M,'myth-legend',3,1850,['oral tradition','ethnographic'],
 "In the folklore of the Zulu and neighbouring peoples of southern Africa, the impundulu or lightning bird is a black-and-white bird the size of a person that summons thunder and lightning with its wings and talons. It is said to be the servant or familiar of a witch, sent to attack the witch's enemies, and to have a vampire-like hunger for blood; sometimes it appears as a handsome young man who seduces women. Where lightning strikes, people have traditionally believed the bird laid an egg or left a trace, and traditional healers have been called on to dig it out or protect homes. The dateNote marks when such beliefs were first written down by outsiders; the tradition itself is older.",
 [('Real birds behind it?','Some writers link the lightning bird to real birds such as the hamerkop, which is surrounded by many beliefs about storms and bad luck in southern Africa; this connection is suggested rather than established.'),
  ('Lightning and witchcraft today','South Africa\'s highveld has some of the highest lightning densities in the world, and in parts of the region lightning deaths are still sometimes blamed on witchcraft, which has led to violence against accused people.'),
  ('A global pattern','Thunderbirds appear in many traditions, including Indigenous North America; the parallel is often noted as a widespread way of explaining storms.')],
 ['wiki:Lightning_bird'],img='Lightning_bird',dateNote='recorded from the 19th century; older oral tradition'),
X('mfecane','The Mfecane: southern Africa\'s time of upheaval and a history war',H,'disputed',2,1815,['textual','oral tradition'],
 "The Mfecane (Zulu, roughly 'the crushing') or Difaqane (Sotho) refers to a period of wars, migrations and new state formation in southern Africa, most intensely from about the 1810s to the 1840s. In this period the Zulu kingdom grew under Shaka, and groups such as the Ndebele under Mzilikazi, the Kololo and the Ngoni moved hundreds or thousands of kilometres, some as far as Zambia, Malawi and Tanzania. New kingdoms, such as Lesotho under Moshoeshoe I and Swaziland (Eswatini), took shape. Traditional estimates of deaths ran from one to two million, but these figures are controversial.",
 [('The traditional view','Older histories, many written by colonial-era authors, described the Mfecane as a chain reaction of destruction started by Shaka\'s Zulu expansion, which left land empty for European settlers.'),
  ('The Cobbing challenge','In 1988 historian Julian Cobbing argued that the Mfecane as usually told was an alibi: that slave and labour raiding linked to the Portuguese at Delagoa Bay and to settlers and missionaries on the Cape frontier drove much of the violence, and that the Zulu-centred story served colonial interests.'),
  ('A middle position','Most historians now see a mix of causes, including drought, competition over trade, internal political change and colonial pressure, and they reject the idea of empty land, while not accepting every point of Cobbing\'s thesis.')],
 ['wiki:Mfecane','wiki:Shaka'],img='Mfecane',end=1840,
 changed="From the 1980s the Mfecane changed from a story of Zulu-caused devastation to a debated, multi-cause period, as historians questioned colonial sources."),
X('thulamela','Thulamela: a gold-working hilltop kingdom in the Kruger Park',H,'confirmed',3,1250,['archaeological','oral tradition'],
 "Thulamela is a stone-walled hilltop site south of the Luvuvhu (Levubu) River in the far north of South Africa's Kruger National Park, one of about 300 archaeological sites in the park. Excavations led by Sidney Miller from 1993 to 1995 found that a community lived there from about the 13th century to the 17th century, working gold, copper and iron into jewellery, hoes and blades. It was linked to the same cultural world and trade networks as Mapungubwe, Great Zimbabwe and Khami, which reached the Indian Ocean coast. Two elite burials with gold ornaments were found and given the names King Ingwe and Queen Losha, and the walls were partly rebuilt for visitors.",
 [('Who were they?','Local Venda and other communities claim connections with Thulamela, and descendants were involved in the reburial of the remains. Archaeologists link it to the wider Zimbabwe tradition but the exact identity of its people is debated.'),
  ('Older myths of foreign builders','Colonial-era writers often claimed that stone-walled sites in southern Africa were built by outsiders such as Phoenicians. Archaeology at sites like Thulamela and Great Zimbabwe shows they were built by African societies, and the foreign-builder idea is rejected.')],
 ['wiki:Thulamela'],img='Thulamela',end=1650,dateNote='c. 13th–17th century'),
X('tikal','Tikal: Maya super-city and the strangers from Teotihuacan (378 CE)',H,'confirmed',2,200,['archaeological','epigraphic','lidar'],
 "Tikal, in the rainforest of northern Guatemala, was one of the most powerful cities of the Classic Maya, with tall temple-pyramids, palaces and a royal dynasty recorded in carved inscriptions. It was settled by about the 1st millennium BCE and flourished roughly 200–900 CE, before being abandoned during the Classic Maya collapse. Inscriptions record that in 378 CE a warlord named Sihyaj K'ahk' arrived, on the same day the local king died, and a new king linked to the distant city of Teotihuacan in central Mexico was installed. In 2018 a lidar survey of the Maya lowlands revealed many previously unknown structures and defences around Tikal. It became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1979, and in 1977 it was filmed as the rebel base in Star Wars.",
 [('Conquest or alliance?','Epigraphers debate whether the 378 entrada was a military takeover by Teotihuacan, an alliance, or the work of a local faction using Teotihuacan symbols for prestige.'),
  ('How many people?','Population estimates for Tikal and its surroundings have risen sharply with lidar, but range widely, from tens of thousands to more than 100,000.')],
 ['wiki:Tikal','wiki:Siyaj Kʼakʼ'],img='Tikal',end=900,dateNote='flourished c. 200–900 CE; entrada 378 CE'),
X('sacred-cenote','The Sacred Cenote of Chichén Itzá and who was really sacrificed',H,'confirmed',2,800,['archaeological','textual','genetic'],
 "The Sacred Cenote is a large natural sinkhole at the Maya city of Chichén Itzá in Yucatán, Mexico. Maya and Spanish colonial sources, including the bishop Diego de Landa, say people threw offerings and human beings into it, especially to the rain god Chaac in times of drought. Between 1904 and 1910 the American consul Edward H. Thompson dredged it and sent gold, jade, copal and human bones to Harvard's Peabody Museum; some objects were later returned to Mexico. Later excavations recovered more remains, including many of children and men.",
 [('The virgin myth','The popular idea that beautiful young women were thrown into the well comes largely from early 20th-century romantic writing. Studies of the bones found a wide range of victims, with many children.'),
  ('What DNA showed','A 2024 genetic study in Nature of 64 children buried in an underground chamber near the cenote found all were boys, many closely related, including two pairs of identical twins, which the authors linked to Maya stories of the Hero Twins. The authors note this was a separate burial site from the cenote itself.'),
  ('Looting or rescue?','Thompson\'s removal of artefacts is now widely viewed as looting, though some at the time saw it as salvage archaeology.')],
 ['wiki:Sacred_Cenote','doi:10.1038/s41586-024-07509-7'],img='Sacred_Cenote',dateNote='used c. 800–1500s CE; dredged 1904–1910'),
X('lost-dutchman-mine',"The Lost Dutchman's Gold Mine, Superstition Mountains",M,'myth-legend',3,1891,['textual','oral tradition'],
 "According to legend, a German immigrant named Jacob Waltz (c. 1810–1891), known as 'the Dutchman', found a fabulously rich gold mine in the Superstition Mountains east of Phoenix, Arizona, and took its location to the grave, leaving only vague clues told on his deathbed. Since then thousands of people have searched for it, and some have died or vanished in the rugged terrain, which added to stories of a curse. The tale grew through newspaper articles, books and later films and television. It is perhaps the most famous lost mine legend in the United States.",
 [('Geology says unlikely','Geologists have long noted that the Superstition Mountains are mainly volcanic rock considered unlikely to contain rich gold veins, though gold was mined elsewhere in the region.'),
  ('Waltz may have had gold','Some researchers think Waltz really did have gold, perhaps from elsewhere or from high-grading at local mines, and that the legend grew around him.'),
  ('Apache traditions','Some versions say the mountains were sacred to the Apache and guarded against outsiders; others tell of earlier Peralta family miners. Historians consider many of these details later inventions.')],
 ["wiki:Lost_Dutchman's_Gold_Mine"],img="Lost_Dutchman's_Gold_Mine",dateNote='Waltz died 1891; legend grew after'),
X('vlad-dracula','Vlad the Impaler: the real prince behind Dracula',H,'confirmed',2,1448,['textual'],
 "Vlad III, Prince of Wallachia (in present-day Romania), ruled three times: briefly in 1448, from 1456 to 1462, and in 1476, when he was killed. His father was a member of the Order of the Dragon, which gave him the name Dracul, so Vlad was called Dracula, 'son of the Dragon'. He fought the Ottoman Empire and rival boyars and became infamous for impaling enemies and captives; pamphlets printed in German lands in the 1460s–1490s spread horror stories about him. In Romania he is often remembered as a defender of the country and a stern enforcer of justice.",
 [('Did he inspire Stoker\'s Dracula?','Bram Stoker\'s 1897 novel took the name Dracula and some historical notes about a Wallachian voivode. Scholars such as Elizabeth Miller argue Stoker knew little about Vlad, and that the popular link between Vlad and the vampire was largely built up by later writers from the 1970s.'),
  ('How cruel was he?','The worst stories come from his enemies, especially German pamphlets and Ottoman chroniclers, so historians debate how much is propaganda, while agreeing he used mass impalement.'),
  ('His tomb','Snagov Monastery has traditionally been named as his burial place, but excavations there did not confirm it, and other proposals, including Comana monastery and a 2014 claim of a tomb in Naples, are not accepted.')],
 ['wiki:Vlad_the_Impaler','wiki:Dracula'],img='Vlad_the_Impaler',end=1476,dateNote='ruled 1448, 1456–1462, 1476'),
X('mercy-brown','Mercy Brown and the New England vampire panic (1892)',H,'confirmed',3,1892,['textual','archaeological'],
 "In Exeter, Rhode Island, the family of George Brown was struck by tuberculosis (then called consumption): his wife and daughter Mary Olive died in the 1880s, and in January 1892 his daughter Mercy died aged 19 while his son Edwin was gravely ill. Neighbours persuaded George that one of the dead was draining the life of the living. In March 1892 the bodies were exhumed; Mercy's, kept in a cold above-ground crypt, appeared little decayed and had blood in the heart. Her heart was burned and the ashes mixed with medicine for Edwin, who died two months later. Newspapers reported the case widely.",
 [('The wider panic','Folklorist Michael Bell and others documented dozens of similar exhumations across New England from the late 18th to the late 19th century, all linked to tuberculosis outbreaks, which spread within families and slowly wasted people away.'),
  ('Why did the body look fresh?','Medical explanations point to the winter cold, which slowed decay; such normal changes in corpses were often taken as signs of vampirism, as in European cases like that of Arnold Paole.'),
  ('Did it inspire Dracula?','Some scholars have suggested that Bram Stoker knew of the case from newspaper reports and drew on it for the character Lucy Westenra in Dracula (1897); this link is plausible but unproven.')],
 ['wiki:Mercy_Brown_vampire_incident','wiki:New_England_vampire_panic'],img='Mercy_Brown_vampire_incident'),
X('man-in-the-iron-mask','The Man in the Iron Mask (died 1703)',H,'confirmed',2,1669,['textual'],
 "The Man in the Iron Mask was a prisoner held in French state prisons from 1669 until his death in the Bastille in 1703, always in the custody of the same jailer, Bénigne Dauvergne de Saint-Mars. Records show he was arrested under the name Eustache Dauger and that his face was hidden from others; contemporary accounts say the mask was black velvet, not iron. His burial was registered under the name Marchioly. The mystery was made famous by Voltaire, and later by Alexandre Dumas's novel The Vicomte of Bragelonne, which made him Louis XIV's twin brother.",
 [('Who was he?','Many historians think he was indeed Eustache Dauger, possibly a valet who knew secrets linked to the imprisoned finance minister Nicolas Fouquet or to political dealings. Other candidates include the Italian diplomat Ercole Antonio Mattioli, whose name resembles the burial record.'),
  ('Royal twin or brother?','Voltaire hinted, and Dumas dramatised, that he was a brother of Louis XIV; there is no evidence for this, though it remains the best-known version.'),
  ('Why so secret?','The extreme secrecy may have been designed to make him seem more important than he was, or to stop him revealing what he knew; the reason remains unknown.')],
 ['wiki:Man_in_the_Iron_Mask'],img='File:Man in the Iron Masque.jpg',end=1703,dateNote='imprisoned 1669–1703'),
]
# --- part 4: early modern & modern mysteries, anomalies, hoaxes and dating science
E+=[
X('princes-in-the-tower','The Princes in the Tower (1483): murder or escape?',H,'disputed',2,1483,['textual','archaeological'],
 "In 1483, after the death of King Edward IV of England, his sons Edward V (aged 12) and Richard, Duke of York (aged 9), were lodged in the Tower of London by their uncle Richard, Duke of Gloucester, ostensibly to prepare for Edward's coronation. Parliament instead declared the boys illegitimate, and their uncle took the throne as Richard III. The princes were seen less and less and then not at all after the summer of 1483. Richard III was killed at Bosworth in 1485 and Henry VII took the throne. In 1674 workmen at the Tower found a box with the bones of two children, which were placed in an urn in Westminster Abbey and examined in 1933; they have not been tested with modern methods.",
 [('Richard III did it','The traditional view, shaped by Thomas More and Shakespeare, is that Richard III ordered their murder, perhaps by Sir James Tyrell; many historians still think this most likely.'),
  ('Other suspects','Other proposed culprits include Henry Stafford, Duke of Buckingham, and Henry VII, who also had reasons to want them dead; supporters of Richard III (Ricardians) argue the case against him rests on hostile Tudor sources.'),
  ('Did they survive?','Pretenders such as Perkin Warbeck claimed to be Prince Richard. In 2023 Philippa Langley\'s Missing Princes Project published documents it says suggest both boys survived; many historians are not persuaded and consider the documents open to other readings.')],
 ['wiki:Princes_in_the_Tower','wiki:Perkin_Warbeck'],img='Princes_in_the_Tower'),
X('nostradamus','Nostradamus and the prophecies that fit anything (1555)',H,'confirmed',2,1555,['textual'],
 "Michel de Nostredame (1503–1566), a French apothecary and astrologer known as Nostradamus, published Les Prophéties in 1555, a collection of four-line verses (quatrains) grouped in 'centuries' of 100. Written in obscure, mixed French with Latin, Greek and anagrams, and almost without dates, they won him fame at the court of Catherine de' Medici. Since then readers have claimed the quatrains foretold events such as the Great Fire of London, the French Revolution, Napoleon, Hitler and the 11 September 2001 attacks. His books have rarely been out of print.",
 [('Believers','Supporters see some quatrains as strikingly precise predictions, for example a verse mentioning Hister, which they read as Hitler.'),
  ('Sceptics and historians','Scholars note that Hister was a classical name for the Danube, that interpretations are almost always made after events, and that the vague language can fit many outcomes; several famous quatrains circulated online are fabricated.'),
  ('Sources','Researchers such as Peter Lemesurier have shown that many verses reworked earlier writings and historical events, using them as patterns for the future.')],
 ['wiki:Nostradamus','wiki:Les_Prophéties'],img='Nostradamus',dateNote='Les Prophéties published 1555'),
X('rasputin-death','The death of Rasputin (1916): poisoned, shot and drowned?',H,'disputed',2,1916,['textual','forensic'],
 "Grigori Rasputin, a Siberian peasant and self-proclaimed holy man, gained great influence at the court of the last Russian tsar, Nicholas II, largely because the empress Alexandra believed he could ease the haemophilia of her son Alexei. Seen as a scandal and a danger during the First World War, he was killed in Petrograd on the night of 29–30 December 1916 (16–17 December in the old Russian calendar) by a group of nobles led by Prince Felix Yusupov. His body was found in the frozen Malaya Nevka river. Less than three months later the monarchy fell.",
 [('The legend of the unkillable monk','Yusupov\'s own memoirs describe Rasputin surviving cyanide-laced cakes and wine and several gunshots before being thrown into the river, and some accounts say he was still alive when he went under water. Historians note that the autopsy reportedly found no poison and attributed death to gunshot wounds, including one to the forehead, so the drowning story is doubtful.'),
  ('British involvement?','Some writers and a 2004 BBC documentary argued that a British intelligence officer, Oswald Rayner, fired the fatal shot because Rasputin was thought to favour peace with Germany. This is debated and the evidence is circumstantial.'),
  ('Healer or charlatan?','Whether Rasputin really eased Alexei\'s bleeding, perhaps by calming the family or by advising against aspirin, remains debated.')],
 ['wiki:Grigori_Rasputin','wiki:Felix_Yusupov'],img='Grigori_Rasputin',dateNote='killed 29–30 December 1916 (New Style)'),
X('ball-lightning','Ball lightning: glowing spheres science still cannot fully explain',H,'disputed',2,1638,['eyewitness','instrumental','experimental'],
 "Ball lightning is a rarely observed phenomenon in which a glowing sphere, usually from a few centimetres to about a metre across, appears during thunderstorms, floats or drifts for seconds, and then fades or explodes. Accounts go back centuries; one of the earliest detailed reports is the Great Thunderstorm at Widecombe-in-the-Moor, England, in 1638, when a ball of fire reportedly entered a church during a service. In July 2012 Chinese scientists studying ordinary lightning on the Tibetan Plateau happened to record a ball lightning event with cameras and spectrometers, and published its spectrum in 2014, which showed elements from the soil such as silicon, iron and calcium.",
 [('Vaporised soil','One leading hypothesis, proposed by John Abrahamson and James Dinniss in 2000, is that lightning striking the ground vaporises silicon and other elements, which then slowly burn in air as a glowing ball; the 2012 observation is consistent with this.'),
  ('Other hypotheses','Other proposals include microwave radiation trapped in a plasma bubble and electrical discharges. Some physicists have suggested that some reports may be hallucinations caused by the magnetic fields of nearby lightning acting on the brain.'),
  ('UFO confusion','Ball lightning and similar luminous phenomena are sometimes suggested as explanations for UFO and ghost light reports, such as the Hessdalen lights; whether they explain specific cases is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Ball_lightning','doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.112.035001','wiki:The_Great_Thunderstorm,_Widecombe'],img='Ball_lightning',dateNote='reported for centuries; Widecombe 1638; recorded 2012'),
X('coso-artifact','The Coso artifact: a "500,000-year-old" spark plug',H,'debunked',3,1961,['physical object'],
 "On 13 February 1961, Wallace Lane, Virginia Maxey and Mike Mikesell were collecting geodes near Olancha, California, when they cut open a stone and found what looked like a metal and porcelain mechanism inside. It was promoted, including in Fortean and creationist literature, as an out-of-place artefact encased in a 500,000-year-old geode, which would imply advanced technology long before humans could make it.",
 [('Identified as a 1920s spark plug','In 1999 investigators Pierre Stromberg and Paul Heinrich, consulting spark plug collectors, identified the object from X-rays as a Champion spark plug of the 1920s. The stone was not a geode but an iron-rich concretion, which can form around objects within decades.'),
  ('Proponents','A few writers still cite the object as an anomaly, noting that it has not been publicly re-examined since its owner withheld it; its current whereabouts are unknown.')],
 ['wiki:Coso_artifact'],img='Coso_artifact',dateNote='found 1961; identified 1999'),
X('aurora-texas-1897','The Aurora, Texas airship crash (1897): an alien buried in a Texas cemetery?',H,'disputed',3,1897,['textual'],
 "On 19 April 1897, during a wave of mystery airship sightings across the United States, the Dallas Morning News printed a report by local correspondent S. E. Haydon that an airship had crashed into Judge J. S. Proctor's windmill in Aurora, Texas, on 17 April. The article said the pilot was 'not an inhabitant of this world', perhaps a Martian, and had been given a Christian burial in the town cemetery. The story was largely forgotten until it was revived in the 1960s and 1970s.",
 [('A newspaper hoax?','The hoax theory rests largely on Aurora resident Etta Pegues, who told Time magazine around 1979–1980 that Haydon wrote the story as a joke to bring interest to the town, which was dying after the railroad bypassed it. She also said Judge Proctor never had a windmill. Many historians regard the story as a tall tale of the 1897 airship craze.'),
  ('Investigations','In 1973 a MUFON team led by Bill Case reported new witnesses and a grave marker that seemed to show a flying object; the cemetery association refused permission to exhume, and the marker later disappeared. MUFON called the evidence inconclusive without ruling out a hoax. Later investigators reported finding the remains of a windmill base near the well and, with ground-penetrating radar, an unmarked grave among 1890s burials; none of this confirms a crash.'),
  ('Part of a pattern','The story is one of many 1896–1897 airship reports, which included both sightings of unexplained lights and many invented newspaper stories.')],
 ['wiki:Aurora,_Texas,_UFO_incident','wiki:Mystery_airship'],img='Aurora,_Texas,_UFO_incident',dateNote='reported 19 April 1897'),
X('gef-talking-mongoose','Gef, the talking mongoose of the Isle of Man (1931)',H,'disputed',3,1931,['eyewitness','textual'],
 "From about 1931 the Irving family, living in a remote farmhouse at Cashen's Gap near Dalby on the Isle of Man, claimed that a creature living in the walls, which called itself Gef and said it was a mongoose born in New Delhi, India, in 1852, spoke to them, sang, swore, told jokes and brought back gossip from the island. The case attracted wide coverage in the British press and visits from psychical researchers, including Harry Price, Hereward Carrington and Nandor Fodor. Price and the journalist R. S. Lambert published The Haunting of Cashen's Gap in 1936. Few outsiders ever claimed to hear or see Gef, and photographs show only a blurry animal.",
 [('Ventriloquism and a lonely teenager','Some investigators at the time and later critics concluded the family, and especially the Irvings\' teenage daughter Voirrey, produced the voice by ventriloquism and other tricks.'),
  ('An imaginary companion?','Media scholar Jeffrey Sconce suggests Gef was most likely an imaginary companion created by the clever teenage daughter; sceptic Joe Nickell says its effects were hyped by family members, reporters and credulous investigators.'),
  ('Belief','Fodor and some later writers took the case seriously as a poltergeist or unexplained phenomenon; it remains a favourite of the paranormal literature.')],
 ['wiki:Gef'],img='Gef',dateNote='from c. 1931'),
X('enfield-poltergeist','The Enfield poltergeist (1977–1979)',H,'disputed',2,1977,['eyewitness','photographic','audio recordings'],
 "Between 1977 and 1979, the Hodgson family in a council house at 284 Green Street, Enfield, north London, reported furniture moving, knocking sounds and objects thrown about, centred on sisters Janet (11) and Margaret (13). Janet appeared to speak in a gruff male voice claiming to be a former occupant who had died in the house. Investigators from the Society for Psychical Research, notably Maurice Grosse and writer Guy Lyon Playfair, spent months there and believed the events were genuine; press photographers and the BBC recorded some incidents.",
 [('Faking','Other SPR investigators, such as Anita Gregory and John Beloff, were unconvinced and found evidence the girls faked some incidents. A video camera caught Janet bending spoons, and Grosse himself saw her banging a broom handle on the ceiling. The sisters admitted some pranks to journalists, while Grosse and Playfair held that other incidents were genuine.'),
  ('Sceptics','Sceptics including stage magician Milbourne Christopher and Joe Nickell argued the investigators were credulous and that the phenomena matched a children\'s hoax; critics say a famous photograph of Janet apparently levitating shows her bouncing off the bed.'),
  ('Cultural impact','The case inspired documentaries and the film The Conjuring 2 (2016), which centres on American paranormal investigators Ed and Lorraine Warren; according to sceptic Brian Dunning their real visit was short.')],
 ['wiki:Enfield_poltergeist'],img='Enfield_poltergeist',end=1979),
X('ourang-medan','SS Ourang Medan: the ghost ship whose crew died staring at the sun',M,'myth-legend',3,1940,['textual'],
 "The Ourang Medan is a ghost ship story first published in Italy and England in 1940 and retold with new details in 1948 and 1952. In the best-known version, set in the Strait of Malacca in 1947 or early 1948, ships pick up a Morse distress call: all officers including the captain are dead, probably the whole crew, and finally 'I die'. The crew of the American ship Silver Star board the vessel and find every crew member, and a dog, dead with their faces frozen in terror, but a fire breaks out and the ship explodes and sinks before it can be towed. In the 1940 version the ship sank south of the Solomon Islands.",
 [('Did the ship exist?','Searches of shipping registers and accident records have found no vessel called Ourang Medan, and the location, date and details change between versions. Many researchers consider it an urban legend; some think it may embellish a real incident.'),
  ('Explanations offered','Writers who think something happened have suggested a cargo of toxic chemicals, carbon monoxide from a faulty boiler, or smuggled nerve agents after the Second World War. Others have proposed paranormal or alien causes. None can be tested without a ship.'),
  ('Sea-mystery patterns','The story shares features with the Mary Celeste and Flying Dutchman: a ship found with its crew gone or dead and no clear cause.')],
 ['wiki:SS_Ourang_Medan'],img='File:Sumatra map.jpg',dateNote='first published 1940; best-known version set in 1947–1948'),
X('radiocarbon-dating-1949','Radiocarbon dating rewrites prehistory (1949)',H,'confirmed',1,1949,['experimental','radiocarbon'],
 "In the late 1940s the American chemist Willard Libby and colleagues showed that the age of once-living material can be measured from its content of carbon-14, a radioactive form of carbon created in the atmosphere that decays with a half-life of about 5,700 years. In 1949 James Arnold and Libby published dates for samples of known age, such as wood from Egyptian tombs, which matched the historical dates. Libby received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1960. Researchers later found that the amount of carbon-14 in the air had varied over time, so radiocarbon dates are now calibrated using tree rings, corals and other records; accelerator mass spectrometry from the late 1970s allowed tiny samples to be dated.",
 [('The radiocarbon revolution','Calibrated dates showed that megalithic tombs in western Europe were older than the Egyptian pyramids. Archaeologist Colin Renfrew argued in Before Civilization (1973) that this overturned the idea that European monuments copied civilisations of the Near East.'),
  ('Limits and controversies','Contamination and reservoir effects can give misleading dates, and results have sparked disputes, such as the 1988 dating of the Shroud of Turin to the Middle Ages, which some still contest.'),
  ('Young-Earth objections','Some young-Earth creationists argue that measurable carbon-14 in coal and diamonds shows these are young. Physicists attribute such traces to contamination and instrument background; the method works only for the last roughly 50,000 years.')],
 ['wiki:Radiocarbon_dating','doi:10.1126/science.110.2869.678','wiki:Willard_Libby'],img='Willard_Libby',
 changed="Before radiocarbon, prehistoric dates depended on guesswork and links to Egyptian chronology; after calibration many sites proved far older than assumed."),
]
